&lt;h2 data-hextra-search-id="the-steps"&gt;The steps&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="the-steps"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#the-steps" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Implement &lt;code&gt;Language&lt;/code&gt; and call &lt;code&gt;analysis.Register&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;init&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Analyze&lt;/code&gt; returns the names a cell defines and uses and its syntax errors
(a &lt;code&gt;Diagnostic&lt;/code&gt; carries a byte offset in the cell). It refuses a source
over &lt;code&gt;lim.MaxBytes&lt;/code&gt;, or nested deeper than &lt;code&gt;lim.MaxDepth&lt;/code&gt;, with an error and
never a panic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Say what crosses to other languages.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;Crosses(owner, user, name)&lt;/code&gt; is the
owner&amp;rsquo;s rule for whether a cell of another language sees &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt;. Within one
language every name is shared. A Go cell sees a Ruby top-level local, not a
Ruby method or constant; a language that cannot hold every name (Go
keywords) implements &lt;code&gt;NameChecker&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark dynamic cells.&lt;/strong&gt; Set &lt;code&gt;Names.Dynamic&lt;/code&gt; when a cell&amp;rsquo;s names cannot be
resolved statically: R&amp;rsquo;s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;assign&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;get&lt;/code&gt; with computed names,
&lt;code&gt;eval(parse())&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;attach&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;library()&lt;/code&gt;. The graph makes a dynamic cell
depend on &lt;strong&gt;every earlier cell&lt;/strong&gt;, and every later cell that reads any name
depend on &lt;strong&gt;it&lt;/strong&gt;, so it runs after anything before it changed and
invalidates whatever may read what it defined.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Say whether the cells run.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;Run{Runs: true}&lt;/code&gt; runs them. Otherwise the
language is &lt;strong&gt;check-only&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;NotRun&lt;/code&gt; is the cell&amp;rsquo;s failure (Julia&amp;rsquo;s names
its milestone M3), and &lt;code&gt;Blocked&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Reads&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;ReadsBlocked&lt;/code&gt; are the
messages of the cells that read it, directly or through other cells.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Optional capabilities&lt;/strong&gt;, found by type assertion:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Interface&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Gives&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;CellAnalyzer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the full &lt;code&gt;Cell&lt;/code&gt; (chunks, funcs, types, display) instead of &lt;code&gt;Names&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Highlighter&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the go-rouge lexer for the HTML export; without it the source is escaped&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Runtime&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the Go module the worker links (import path, module, default version); the kernel adds it to the work module&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;TypeLearner&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;variables typed when the cell has run, as Ruby&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Register the run backend&lt;/strong&gt; for a language that runs, with
&lt;code&gt;gen.RegisterBackend(name, backend)&lt;/code&gt; from your own package. &lt;code&gt;Infer&lt;/code&gt;
declares the cell&amp;rsquo;s variables, &lt;code&gt;Write&lt;/code&gt; writes its function and &lt;code&gt;Runtime&lt;/code&gt;
names the import and the prelude; the imported package comes from the
language&amp;rsquo;s &lt;code&gt;Runtime&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;code&gt;gen.Context&lt;/code&gt; it receives exposes the cell, its
inputs, the declared variables and type resolution. No edit of &lt;code&gt;gen&lt;/code&gt; is
needed; Ruby&amp;rsquo;s backend (&lt;code&gt;gen/ruby.go&lt;/code&gt;) is the model.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h2 data-hextra-search-id="testing"&gt;Testing&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="testing"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#testing" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Test with a fake language that implements only &lt;code&gt;Language&lt;/code&gt;, as
&lt;code&gt;analysis/lang_test.go&lt;/code&gt; does, and fuzz the dispatch with unknown or hostile
fence words (&lt;code&gt;FuzzDispatch&lt;/code&gt;). The project keeps 100 % coverage.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>